   "id": "ae6b7f28-fc66-4c3f-a095-f1e52f30ba1a",
   "term": "Solder Joint Inspection",
   "verdict": "MINOR",
   "suggestions": [
    {
     "field": "definition",
     "severity": "Medium",
     "issue": "The entry teaches that a dull/grainy joint indicates a defect ('defects appear dull, grainy'; plain_english repeats it). Web-verified via I-Connect007 and Stellar Technical Products: lead-free (SAC tin/silver/copper) joints normally solidify with a matte/dull/grainy surface, and this is 'an effect, not a defect.' Since most modern manufactured audio gear uses lead-free solder, a student applying the shiny=good/dull=bad rule will wrongly condemn sound lead-free joints.",
     "suggestion": "Qualify the appearance criterion: note that the shiny=good vs dull=defect rule applies to leaded (Sn/Pb) solder, and that lead-free (SAC) joints are normally duller/grainier by nature. Judge lead-free joints by wetting, fillet shape, and absence of cracks/voids rather than shine."
    }
   ]
